  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 250 AM CDT Thu Aug 27 2026\n\nAn amplified, meridional mid-level pattern remains in place over the \nCONUS with a ridge axis over the western US and troughs over the \neastern US and just off the Pacific Northwest coastline. A shortwave \nin the northwest flow is generating a complex of storms in far \nsouthwestern KS. That rain should stay well to our west today. At \nthe surface, high pressure will continue to build in from the north \nbehind the weak cold front that moved through the area yesterday. A \nslightly drier air mass with the ridge means lower dew points than \nrecent days. The lower dew points will make for a few pleasant \nmornings with lows in the low 60s early today and again tonight into \nearly Friday. Afternoon temperatures, however, will remain fairly \nwarm in the upper 80s to near 90 degrees for most, especially near \nand south of I-70.\n\nAnother shortwave will likely trigger storms in western Nebraska \nthis evening. A few CAMs show an associated MCS moving southward \ninto north-central KS and possibly clipping the far western CWA \ntonight, but there is not enough consistency from models to go much \nhigher than slight chance POPs. South winds increase on Friday as \nthe surface ridge moves east of the area and low pressure deepens \nover the Front Range and High Plains. Temperatures respond to \nstronger southerly flow and height rises aloft this weekend. Highs \ntop out in the mid 90s on Saturday and could approach 100 degrees on \nSunday. The heat index will be a few degrees higher with dew points \nin the upper 60s. Overnight lows will also be very warm, mainly in \nthe mid to upper 70s. See the climate section below for a look at \nthe record highest minimum temperatures for Sunday and Monday.\n\nA surface trough axis will sit across central KS this weekend, but \nweak forcing and a strong cap should keep conditions dry. \nTemperatures will remain above average heading into September with \nlow POPs early next week, mainly during nighttime hours.",
